<h1>Low-Profile Conveyors: Compact &amp; Knife-Edge Designs</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Low-profile conveyors minimize overall height to fit under equipment, integrate at ergonomic workstation heights, and enable transfers between closely spaced machines. These compact <a href="/conveyor-types/belt-conveyors/">belt conveyors</a> use thin belts, small-diameter nose bars, and compact drives to save vertical space. Dorner, for example, describes its <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/1100-series" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1100 Series</a> as having a 3/4 inch frame height. Knife-edge versions transfer small products across very small gaps.</p>
<h2 id="features-heading">Low-Profile Design Elements</h2>
<h3>Minimal Frame Height</h3>
<p>Low-profile conveyors use thin extruded aluminum or sheet metal frames rather than bulky structural sections. Frame heights of a few inches or less are common, and the smallest miniature conveyors have frames under an inch tall. Check whether a quoted height is the frame alone or includes the drive, which often hangs below or beside the frame.</p>
<h3>Small-Diameter Pulleys</h3>
<p>Small end pulleys reduce the conveyor's end height. Dorner's 1100 Series, for example, uses a 1 inch drive pulley on end-drive models and 0.625 inch idler pulleys. General-purpose conveyors use noticeably larger pulleys. Smaller pulleys require belts designed for tight bend radii (timing belts, thin urethane belts).</p>
<h3>Knife-Edge Transfer</h3>
<p>A knife-edge nose bar creates a sharp discharge point that shrinks the gap between the conveyor and downstream equipment. Published examples: Dorner offers a 0.31 inch (8 mm) nose bar on the 1100 Series and a 5/8 inch high-speed nose bar on the <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/2200-series/2200-belted-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2200 Series</a> rated to 200 fpm. QC Conveyors offers an optional nosebar tail with an 11 mm radius on its <a href="https://qcconveyors.com/low-profile-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">low-profile Automation Series</a>. Small nose bars typically cap belt speed and load, so check the rating for the nose bar, not just the conveyor. Used for small bottles, vials, cartons, and blister packs.</p>
<h3>End Drive vs. Center Drive</h3>
<p><strong>End drive</strong> places the motor at one end, adding to overall length but keeping the profile thin. <strong>Center/under-belt drive</strong> mounts the drive below the belt, keeping both ends free for transfers but adding depth under the frame. Choose based on whether length or height is the critical constraint.</p>
<h3>Belt Selection</h3>
<p>Low-profile conveyors require belts that handle tight pulley diameters without damage:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Timing belts:</strong> Positive drive, tight radius capability, precise positioning</li>
<li><strong>Thin urethane belts:</strong> flexible, good grip</li>
<li><strong>Flat belts:</strong> Thin polyester or nylon carcass</li>
<li><strong>Round belts:</strong> Multiple parallel round belts for light loads</li>
</ul>
