<h2 id="applications-heading">Low-Profile Applications</h2>
<h3>Machine Integration</h3>
<p>Low-profile conveyors integrate under packaging machines where standard conveyors won't fit. Applications include:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">Infeed and outfeed</a> under labelers, printers, and inspection systems</li>
<li>Under case erectors and case packers with limited clearance</li>
<li>Between closely spaced equipment with minimal gap</li>
<li>Under shrink tunnels and heat sealers</li>
</ul>
<h3>Pharmaceutical and Medical</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">Pharmaceutical packaging</a> uses low-profile and knife-edge conveyors for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Vial and ampoule transfer between filling and capping</li>
<li>Blister pack handling through packaging lines</li>
<li>Carton and leaflet transfer to cartoners</li>
<li><a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/cleanroom/">Cleanroom applications</a> where compact design aids cleaning</li>
</ul>
<h3>Food and Beverage</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food packaging</a> applications include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Small bottle and jar transfer in tight spaces</li>
<li>Pouch and flexible package handling</li>
<li>Integration under <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">tabletop chain conveyors</a></li>
<li>Snack bag and small carton transfer</li>
</ul>
<h3>Workstation Integration</h3>
<p>Low-profile conveyors integrate with assembly and inspection workstations at ergonomic heights. Products flow through work areas without requiring operators to reach up or down. Common in electronics assembly, medical device manufacturing, and quality inspection stations.</p>
<h2 id="evaluate-heading">How to Evaluate Options</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Height definition:</strong> Ask whether the stated height is frame only or frame plus drive, and where the drive sits (end, center, or side).</li>
<li><strong>Nose bar rating:</strong> Confirm the speed and load rating with the nose bar option you need. Small nose bars often have lower limits than the base conveyor.</li>
<li><strong>Transfer gap:</strong> Give the supplier your smallest product length and footprint so they can size the nose bar, transfer plate, or driven transfer.</li>
<li><strong>Load and speed:</strong> State product weight, units per minute, and whether product accumulates on the belt. Miniature frames have much lower load ratings than small-frame conveyors.</li>
<li><strong>Belt compatibility:</strong> Confirm the belt material is rated for the pulley and nose bar diameters, and for food or cleanroom contact if needed.</li>
<li><strong>Guarding:</strong> Small nose bars and pinch drives still create nip points. Guarding must meet <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">29 CFR 1910.212</a> and <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Integration:</strong> Ask for mounting options to the host machine, stand heights, and controls interface (start/stop, speed reference).</li>
</ul>
<p>See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">conveyor manufacturers directory</a> for supplier listings.</p>
