<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a low-profile conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A low-profile conveyor is a <a href="/conveyor-types/belt-conveyors/">belt conveyor</a> with a short frame height, small end pulleys, and a compact drive. It fits under or between existing equipment, mounts on workstations, and transfers products between closely spaced machines. Frame height is not floor height: the conveyor still sits on stands or a machine bed at the working height you need.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a knife-edge conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A knife-edge conveyor uses a very small diameter nose bar at the transfer end so products cross to the next conveyor or machine over a small gap. Published examples include Dorner's 0.31 inch (8 mm) nose bar on the 1100 Series and its 5/8 inch high-speed nose bar on the 2200 Series, rated to 200 fpm. Smaller nose bars usually limit speed and load and need thin, flexible belts. Common in <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">pharmaceutical</a>, electronics, and small parts packaging.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How thin can a conveyor be?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Very thin. Dorner describes its 1100 Series as having a 3/4 inch frame height, with an optional 0.31 inch (8 mm) nose bar, loads up to 15 lbs, and belt speeds up to 80 fpm. Thinner frames trade away load and speed capacity. The practical minimum depends on belt type, product weight, speed, and whether the drive can hang below the frame or must sit at one end.</p>

</div>
